    "physical_description": "Watercolour with ink on a prepared red ground, depicting a young man standing, holding a gentleman’s cane in his right hand. The red square sheet has been collaged at a diagonal onto a white backing sheet. The image has sections blocked in white with details in black linework and stippling, and some parts coloured in yellow. The man depicted is light-skinned and dressed formally in a black top hat and a <i>barong</i> with a standing collar and four buttons; it has a checkered pattern made with white squares and lines which contrast with the red of the ground showing through. He also wears white trousers and a pair of yellow shoes. Underneath the image is a white, oval-shaped caption block; on this are five lines of verse lettered in black, written in a Filipino language. At the top of the red sheet is a marking which reads, ‘B/ 3’. ",
    "summary": "This watercolour, made by an unknown artist from the Philippines, is among the earliest objects to enter the V&A’s collection. One of eight by the same hand, the works directly correspond to another set (528 to 535), which were made at a similar time.\r\n\r\nThe artworks relate to a genre called<i> tipos del país</i> (‘types of the country’), popularised in nineteenth-century Manila. These small watercolours, depicting local people in ‘typical’ dress, were fashionable souvenirs for foreign traders in the latter part of the Spanish colonial period (1565–1898). Conventionally painted on white paper, this example reveals an unusual technique, with vivid colours on a prepared red ground.\r\n\r\n<i>Tipos del país</i> were often lettered with titles that flattened subjects into reductive socio-ethnic categories. The Tagalog language captions here are untypical, offering a distinctive counterpoint. In lines of rhyming verse, they add a playful tone and more nuanced, often humorous, layer of personality. Some words are transliterated using Spanish spellings, which, along with the mixing of native and European fashions, exposes legacies of Empire in the form and fabric of these objects. The works evidence Manila as a cosmopolitan Southeast Asian capital – and a complex fusion of East and West – in the 1800s, as it is today. ",
